# Setting up a tracker

You set up each tracker once, as a **definition**: what it is, how long it
should last, and when to remind you. After that you start and complete it as
often as you change the thing it tracks.

Definitions live under **Settings > Notifications & Trackers**, on the
**Definitions** tab. Press **New Definition** to make one, or the pencil icon on
a row to edit one.

<Screenshot id="tracker-editor" />

## The basics

| Field | What it does |
|-------|--------------|
| **Name** | What you call it: "CGM sensor", "Left site", "Endocrinologist". Shown on the dashboard pill and in notifications. |
| **Category** | What kind of thing it is. This sets the icon and the reasons you can pick when you complete it, and **Reservoir** adds a [low insulin alert](#reservoir-trackers). |
| **Description** | Optional. For your own reference. |
| **Tracker Mode** | **Duration** counts up from when you start it. Use it for anything you wear or use up. **Event** counts down to a date and time you set. Use it for appointments, refills and other booked things. |
| **Expected Lifespan** | Duration trackers only. How long this thing is meant to last. Type `10d` for ten days, or `10x24` for ten lots of 24 hours. |

The lifespan draws the progress line on the dashboard pill and the "time
remaining" figure. It is also the "end" that a
[before-the-end reminder](#notification-thresholds) counts back from. Use the
wear time from the manufacturer's instructions, or the one your care team has
given you. A tracker does not know how long your sensor or site is actually
approved for.

## Restarting automatically

A duration tracker can restart by itself when your pump, loop or a connector
reports a change. Under **Restart automatically on**, tick the events that mean
this thing was changed: `Sensor Start` for a CGM sensor, `Site Change` or
`Cannula Change` for an infusion site, `Pod Change` for a pod, and so on.

<Screenshot id="tracker-triggers" />

Once you tick an event, a **notes** box appears. Leave it empty unless two of
your trackers listen for the same event. Then give each one a word from the
event's notes, such as "left" and "right", so each only restarts on its own
changes.

Which events arrive depends on your devices and apps, so check a tracker
restarts the first time you make a change. See
[automatic restarts](/docs/trackers/everyday-use#automatic-restarts) for what
happens when one does.

## Notification thresholds

A threshold is one reminder step. Each has:

- **Level**: Info, Warning, Hazard or Urgent. This decides how prominent the
  reminder is, and how it looks on the dashboard.
- **After (hours)**, or **Hours** for an event tracker: when it fires. For a duration tracker, a positive number counts
  from the start and a negative number counts back from the end of the
  lifespan. `-24` on a ten-day sensor means "24 hours before day ten". For an
  event tracker, a negative number is before the event and a positive number is
  after.
- **Description**: the message the notification carries. Leave it blank and
  Nocturne makes one, such as "24h before end".

<Screenshot id="tracker-thresholds" />

You can add up to four. A common ladder for a sensor looks like the example
above: a notice the day before it ends, a warning shortly before, and an urgent
alert once it is overdue.

A before-the-end step needs a lifespan, and must be shorter than it. Change the
lifespan later and every before-the-end step moves with it.

### Where the notification goes

Each threshold becomes an **alert rule**, and shows up on the **Alerts** page
alongside your glucose alerts, named after the tracker and the step. That rule
is what delivers it, so a tracker reminder works like any other alert: you can
choose its channels, it appears in the alert history, and you can acknowledge
it.

A new step is delivered inside Nocturne. To have it reach your phone, or any
other channel, save the tracker, reopen it, and press **Channels** next to the
step. That opens its alert rule. Your channel choices stay in place when you
edit the tracker later. Only removing the step removes its rule.

A threshold only fires while its tracker is running. Completing the tracker
ends any reminder it has raised.

<Callout type="warning" title="Do not rely on one reminder">

  A reminder can arrive late or not at all: your phone may be offline,
  notifications may be muted, or the server may be down. For anything where a
  missed change would be a problem, keep a second reminder somewhere else, and
  keep your device's own alarms on.

</Callout>

## On the dashboard

**Dashboard Visibility** decides when the tracker shows as a pill on the home
screen:

- **Always show**: whenever it is running.
- **Show after Info threshold**, **Warn**, **Hazard** or **Urgent**: only once
  it has reached that step. This keeps long-running things like a pump battery
  out of the way until they need attention.
- **Off**: never. It still runs and still sends its notifications.

## Who can see it

**Public Visibility** controls whether other people who sign in to your site,
such as a family member you have invited, can see this tracker running.
**Private**, the default, keeps it to your own account. Trackers are never
shown on a [public share link](/docs/sharing/public-link), whichever you pick.

## Writing events for Nightscout apps

Under **Event Integration**, a tracker can create a treatment when you start it,
when you complete it, or both. Pick an event type such as `Sensor Start`,
`Site Change` or `Insulin Change`. Leave it on **None** to create nothing.

Turn this on if something else still reads your Nightscout treatments and
expects to see those events. For example, a phone app with its own sensor age
display, or anything using the legacy `/api/v1/treatments` endpoint. If nothing
reads them, you can leave it off.

## Reservoir trackers

Choose the **Reservoir** category and two more fields appear:

- **Low reservoir alert (units)**: warn when the estimated insulin left in the
  pump drops below this many units.
- **Level alert urgency**: how prominent that alert is.

This is separate from the time-based thresholds. A reservoir tracker can remind
you after three days and warn when it is getting low, whichever comes first.

Nocturne estimates the insulin left from the most recent exact level it has,
minus the insulin delivered since. That level comes from your pump or loop, if
it reports one, or from **Record Level** on the tracker. Insulin delivered
means boluses and basal. Some pumps only report a rough level, such as "50+".
Nocturne treats that as "at least 50", so it will not raise a low alert from
it. A level more than 14 days old is ignored. If Nocturne has no recent level
at all, the low reservoir alert stays quiet rather than guessing.

<Callout type="warning" title="An estimate, not a measurement">

  The estimate can drift. Priming, a leak, a missed upload, or insulin given
  outside the pump are all invisible to it. Your pump's own reading is the one
  to trust. Recording a level after you refill (**Record Level** > **Fresh
  fill**) gives the estimate a fresh starting point.

</Callout>

## Presets

A preset is a saved way of starting a tracker, with notes filled in. Make them
on the **Presets** tab with **New Preset**. Give it a name, pick the tracker,
and optionally write the start notes it should use.

Presets suit anything you rotate. For example, "Sensor, left arm" and "Sensor,
right arm", both starting the same sensor tracker. The history then shows which
site each sensor was on.

## Deleting a definition

Deleting a definition deletes its history too, along with its alert rules.
There is one exception for the rules. If another of your alert rules is built
on one of them, such as an escalation that fires when a tracker reminder goes
unanswered, the tracker's rule is kept but switched off. That way the rule built
on it visibly stops, instead of quietly never firing again.

To stop using a tracker but keep its history, complete it, set its dashboard
visibility to **Off**, and don't start it again.
